      <Byline post={post} />

      <p>
        Hinge’s slogan is four words long: <em>designed to be deleted</em>. It
        may be the only advertising in the world that promises you a funeral.
        Take it seriously for a moment, because it is a strange thing for a
        subscription business to say. A subscription earns nothing from an
        ending. It earns from the middle, from the long undecided stretch where
        you are still looking, still hoping, still paying. A dating app that
        worked the way its slogan claims would be a poor business, and the
        company appears to know it: Match Group’s own chief executive has told
        staff that Tinder and Hinge users feel the company is too driven by
        metrics. The slogan and the memo are both sincere. Holding them
        together is what this essay is for.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some numbers first, so the argument has weight to push against. Match
        Group owns more than forty dating brands, Tinder, Hinge, OkCupid and
        Plenty of Fish among them, and about half the dating activity of young
        Americans runs through its servers. Tinder Plus launched at $9.99 a
        month and now costs $24.99. Bumble’s paid tier has tripled since 2016.
        The League, at the luxury end, will take up to $2,499.99 a month.
        Things that used to be free now sit behind those prices: seeing who
        liked you, filtering by distance, more than a handful of swipes a day.
        And on Valentine’s Day 2024, six users filed a federal class action
        alleging what the pricing already implies. The apps, the complaint
        says, are gamified on purpose, tuned like slot machines to keep you
        searching for rewards the operator keeps just out of reach. Match
        called the suit ridiculous
        and without merit. Perhaps. But the design it describes needs no
        conspiracy. It is simply what the incentives ask for.
      </p>

      <h2>The meter</h2>
      <p>
        Here is the mechanism with no villain in it. A dating company is paid
        for access to matches, so its inventory is the distance between you and
        the person you are trying to reach. When a match succeeds, the firm
        loses two subscribers; a near miss keeps both, and the last inch can be
        sold. Nobody in the building has to want this; it
        falls out of a quarterly target the way water falls downhill. Scarcity
        is stock, frustration is turnover, and the perfect customer is the one
        who almost finds someone, forever.
      </p>
      <p>
        Economists have a name for what happens next. In a market where buyers
        cannot tell good from bad, the good options leave first and the market
        sours from the top; George Akerlof called it the market for lemons.
        Dating apps run the experiment on people. Put the likeliest matches
        behind a paywall and the people most worth meeting are the first to
        give up and go. Each departure lowers the odds for everyone still
        swiping, which sends the next tier out of the door behind them. The
        paywall does not just tax the market; it curdles it. Nearly four in
        five Gen Z daters report dating-app fatigue, and fatigue is what
        curdling feels like from the inside.
      </p>

      <h2>The matchmaker</h2>
      <p>
        So how did people meet before, in the ways they remember warmly?
        Almost never unaided; that part of the nostalgia is false. There was
        nearly always an intermediary. In the Jewish villages of eastern
        Europe it was the shadchan, a professional whose trade was knowing
        every family’s character and circumstances and proposing marriages
        between them. Elsewhere it was an aunt, a church social, a barn dance,
        or the slow evening walk that Mediterranean towns still call the
        paseo. Even the personals column of a local newspaper was an
        intermediary of a kind: it took a flat fee for the advert and had no
        further stake in your search.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look at what those forms shared, because it was not innocence. First,
        recurrence: you would see these people again next week, so patience
        was possible and reputations mattered. Second, the format absorbed the
        no. In a dance where everyone rotates, not being chosen is a fact of
        the room rather than a verdict on your face. Third, contact cost
        nothing; where admission existed it was flat and cheap. Fourth, and
        this is the load-bearing one, the intermediary was paid for the
        outcome, not the search. A shadchan lived on reputation in a village
        that would judge the marriage for decades. The aunt answered to both
        families at every holiday for the rest of her life. Bad matches cost
        the matchmaker, which is precisely the liability no swipe app carries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be honest about the price of all this. These were also the mechanisms
        of small worlds that could suffocate: chaperones, narrow pools,
        communities that punished difference and made leaving expensive.
        Recurrence is the warm name for a room you cannot easily exit. The
        point is not that the village was better, only that its machinery was
        aligned — and the machinery can be taken without the walls.
      </p>
      <p>
        The historian Beth Bailey dated the turn precisely. Until the 1920s,
        American courtship ran on the calling system: a man visited a woman in
        her family’s parlour, at her family’s invitation, and the whole
        apparatus cost nothing and answered to her side. Within a generation,
        courtship moved out of the parlour and into restaurants, theatres and
        dance halls, and access to those rooms had to be bought. Money, men’s
        money, became the medium of courtship for the first time. Everything
        since has been elaboration. The apps did not invent the meter. They
        shrank it, put it in your pocket, and pointed it at the introduction
        itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Disintermediating your friends</h2>
      <p>
        The scale of the change has been measured. Stanford’s long-running
        survey of how American couples meet found that 39 per cent of
        heterosexual couples met online by 2017, up from 22 per cent in 2009.
        Online passed friends as the leading way around 2013, and for same-sex
        couples the figure was already about 65 per cent. The researchers’
        paper carries the honest title: <em>Disintermediating Your Friends</em>.
        A friend who introduces you is infrastructure: they know you both,
        filter better than any questionnaire, stake a little of their own
        standing on the introduction, and do it for free. Worse: for joy. The
        subscription sells you back their labour, minus the knowledge, minus
        the stake.
      </p>

      <h2>Where people are going instead</h2>
      <p>
        Watch what people run to when they leave, because it is a map of what
        was missing. Attendance at in-person singles events grew by 42 per
        cent in a single year. Timeleft seats strangers around dinner tables
        every Wednesday in three hundred cities, and the unit is the table,
        not the pair, so nobody at it can be rejected. Run clubs are the paseo
        with cardio: recurring, free, no declared intent required. Board-game
        cafés hand you a conversation so you do not have to build one from
        nothing. Speed dating is back, and it is the dance hall’s rotation
        with name tags. There is even a $25 ring, the Pear ring, whose only
        feature is announcing that you are single and approachable — the
        ambient signal a promenade used to provide for free. Notice the
        melancholy detail under the revival, though: nearly all of it is
        ticketed. The sociologist Ray Oldenburg called the free rooms of a
        town its third places, and most of them have closed. Their
        reconstruction charges at the door.
      </p>
      <p>
        Online, the interesting experiments are the ones almost nobody
        covers. Some people now write date-me docs: long, earnest,
        self-authored pages that circulate link by link and gather in a small
        volunteer directory. Lex, a queer personals app descended from the
        back-page adverts of a 1980s magazine, puts the text first and the
        selfies second. The Marriage Pact asks students fifty questions about
        values, runs a Nobel-winning stable-matching algorithm over the
        answers, and returns one match, once a year, free, with no
        photographs. More than six hundred thousand students across a hundred
        campuses have taken it. And an uncountable number of couples simply
        met in a Discord server or a <em>Final Fantasy XIV</em> raid group; by
        the time either of them thought of it as dating, they had spent two
        hundred hours solving hard problems together. The pattern across every one
        of these is the same: no meter, and a high cost of authorship. You
        cannot swipe through three-thousand-word documents, and there is no
        way to buy a raid group’s esteem. Effort filters better than money,
        because effort selects for the thing you are actually trying to find.
      </p>

      <h2>The honest objection</h2>
      <p>
        If the apps were simply collapsing under their own cynicism, this
        essay could end here, and it would be wrong. Tinder’s paying users
        fell from about eleven million to under nine in three years, and
        Bumble cut nearly a third of its staff. Hinge, meanwhile, grew
        subscribers by 17 per cent and revenue by 26 per cent. The market
        is not punishing the metered model. It is rewarding whoever performs
        intentionality most convincingly, and performing intentionality is
        permanently cheaper than being aligned to it, because alignment costs
        revenue and performance costs a brand campaign. That is the actual
        problem, and no redesign of the feed can reach it, because it lives in
        who pays whom, for what.
      </p>

      <h2>What we built, and what we refused</h2>
      <p>
        The test of an argument about incentives is what you do when you hold
        the other end of one. <a href="/">xNet</a> is a workspace, not a
        dating service, but it carries a people-matching layer, and building
        it forced every one of these choices onto the table. The shape we
        chose: your profile is derived, not performed. The system already
        holds a graph of what you care about, so a shy person never has to
        write an advert for themselves. Contact is a wave: each side signals
        interest privately, stored as a salted hash even the relaying server
        cannot read, and only when both have waved does an introduction open.
        Nobody messages you cold, and there is no inbox to buy your way into.
        And romance is one intent among seven, alongside friendship,
        collaborators, mentorship and the rest — facets of one primitive
        rather than an app of its own. That last choice matters more than it
        looks. Carving romance off into a separate product with its own
        growth target is how the meter got built the first time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Honesty about the gap, as ever: what ships today is the local half —
        the derived profile, the wave, friends-of-friends discovery. The
        federated directory that would let strangers find each other across
        communities is designed and not yet built, and the moderation bill
        that comes with it is a real cost that the paywall, for all its sins,
        currently pays elsewhere. We would rather tell you
        that plainly than imply a dating service exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        The part worth writing home about is the part we made impossible. Our
        continuous-integration gate, the same one that already fails any
        build containing a streak counter or an infinite scroll, now also
        fails any build that introduces a metered connection. An identifier
        like <code>boostPrice</code>, <code>paidVisibility</code> or
        <code>payToReveal</code> stops the merge. The refused-rents list in
        our <a href="/commitments">public charter</a> carries the entry in
        plain words: no rent on introductions, ever, with a test pinning the
        receipt. A promise with a lint rule is a different kind of promise.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a deeper reason the good version has to be unmonetised
        rather than differently monetised. Suppose you tried to rebuild the
        shadchan’s incentive honestly and charge only for outcomes. A
        matchmaker paid on the marriage has to observe the marriage. In a
        village the observer was a neighbour; at software scale it is
        surveillance, and the cure is worse than the fee. Which leaves one
        honest place for connection to live: as a feature of ground you
        already own, riding a flat bill for hosting, worth nothing extra to
        the operator either way. The matchmaker cannot be
        a business. It can be infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Designed to be deleted</em> was always the right idea in the
        wrong tense: an aspiration wearing the clothes of a description. The
        version that can actually be built is older than the apps and plainer
        than the slogan — a matchmaker whose incentives face the couple, a
        room you will be in again next week, a no that lands on the format
        instead of on your face, and a coin that stays in your pocket,
        because nothing on the far side of the glass was ever scarce to begin
        with.
      </p>

